_m$ algebra.\n\nComments:  | Improved exposition and minor corrections\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Quantum Algebra (math.QA)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2504.00834 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2504.00834v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.00834 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Apratim Chakraborty [view email]\n[v1] Tue, 1 Apr 2025 14:24:55 UTC (367 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 27 Oct 2026 02:03:29 UTC (259 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled A spanning tree model for chromatic homology, by Aninda Banerjee and 3 other authors\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA spanning tree model for chromatic homology | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2504.00834)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; The resolution of the Homological Span and Torsion Existence conjectures clarifies long-standing questions about the structural properties of these homology groups.\n## Torsion in $A_m$A m​ Chromatic Homology\n\nAnother significant result is the resolution of the \"Torsion Existence Conjecture\" for chromatic homology over the algebra $A_m = \\mathbb{Z}[x]/\\langle x^m \\rangle$A m​=Z[x]/⟨x m⟩. This algebra generalizes the standard $A_2$A 2​ case. The conjecture, posed by Helme-Guizon, Przytycki, and Rong, specifies the conditions under which the homology groups contain torsion.\n\nThe authors prove that for $m \\ge 2$m≥2:\n\n  1. The homology $H_{A_m}^*(G)$H A m​∗​(G) contains torsion if and only if $G$G has no loops and contains at least one cycle of length $\\ge 3$≥3.\n  2. The order of this torsion must divide $m$m.\n\nTo prove this, the authors analyze the differential $\\partial_{ST}$∂S T​ of the spanning tree complex. They construct a specific cycle in the complex and show that its boundary is $m$m times a non-exact critical state. This explicit construction demonstrates that $m$m acts as a null-homologous coefficient, confirming the existence of torsion of order $m$m (or a divisor thereof).\n\n## The Combinatorial Differential\n\nThe paper provides an explicit combinatorial formula for the differential of the spanning tree complex over $A_2$A 2​. This is a major step toward practical implementation, as it allows one to compute the homology without building the full subgraph complex.\n\nFor two critical states $T$T and $T'$T′, the incidence in the differential is non-zero only if $T'$T′ is obtained from $T$T by a specific edge swap. Let $e$e be an external edge relative to $T$T, and let $f$f be the maximal internally live edge in the unique cycle created by $T \\cup \\{e\\}$T∪{e}. If $T' = (T \\cup \\{e\\}) \\setminus \\{f\\}$T′=(T∪{e})∖{f}, the incidence $[\\partial_{ST}(T): T']$[∂S T​(T):T′] is calculated based on the parity of \"live\" edges along paths in the tree.\n\nThe incidence formula is given as:\n\n$$(-1)^{\\xi(T,e)} ((-1)^{L(P_{v1}(T))} + (-1)^{L(P_{v2}(T))})$$(−1)ξ(T,e)((−1)L(P v 1​(T))+(−1)L(P v 2​(T)))\n\nwhere:\n\n  * $\\xi(T,e)$ξ(T,e) is the number of inactive edges in $G$G smaller than $e$e.\n  * $L(P_{vi}(T))$L(P v i​(T)) counts the number of live edges on the paths from a fixed root to the endpoints of edge $e$e.\n\nThis results in coefficients that are restricted to the set $\\{0, 2, -2\\}${0,2,−2}, providing a clear explanation for why torsion of order 2 is so prevalent in chromatic homology over $A_2$A 2​.\n\n\nThis is the foundational paper that first introduced chromatic homology, which is the central mathematical object studied in the main paper. The authors develop their spanning tree model as a new, more computationally efficient way to understand the chromatic complex originally defined in this work.\n\n[HGR05] L. Helme-Guizon and Y. Rong. A categorification for the chromatic polynomial. Algebraic and Geometric Topology, 2005.\n\nA broken circuit model for chromatic homology theories\n\nThis paper is a direct methodological precursor to the work being reviewed, as it also applied algebraic Morse theory to simplify the chromatic complex. The current paper explicitly states that it builds on the work of Chandler and Sazdanovic, further reducing their complex of NBC subgraphs to one generated only by NBC spanning trees.\n\n[CS19] Alex Chandler and Radmila Sazdanovic. A broken circuit model for chromatic homology theories, 2019.\n\nPatterns in khovanov link and chromatic graph homology\n\nThis paper posed a key conjecture about the homological span of chromatic homology over the algebra A_m. The main paper's spanning tree model is directly applied to prove this conjecture, making it a central motivation and a major claimed result of the research.\n\n[SS18] Radmila Sazdanovic and Daniel Scofield. Patterns in khovanov link and chromatic graph homology, 2018.\n\nTorsion in graph homology\n\nThis paper put forth a conjecture concerning the existence and order of torsion in the chromatic homology over A_m algebras. Proving this conjecture is the second major application of the spanning tree model presented in the main paper, highlighting the model's utility in resolving open questions.\n\n[LHG06] Yongwu Rong Laure Helme-Guizon, J´ozef H. Przytycki. Torsion in graph homology. Fundamenta Mathematica, 190(1):139–177, 2006.\n\n48 views\n\nv1\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications | Faculty of Mathematics (https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/publications?page=16)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * If you specify more than one search option (e.g. you search for both \"Authors\" and \"Paper title\") then the publications returned will be those that match all of your search terms ... Learn from your own latents and not from tokens: A sample-complexity theory ... (doi: 10.48550/arxiv.2503.12223) ... Remarks on the disproof of the unit distance conjecture ... Correction: Analysis of a