ode: 2539.05290 Lev.code: 29736000 GTIN/EAN: 04005176043604\n\n€ 132,85\n\nInloggen Registreer of login en zie direct uw netto prijzen.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Saxl hypergraph of a permutation group (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391911133_The_Saxl_hypergraph_of_a_permutation_group)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2505.13849 ... In particular, we consider groups with complete Saxl hypergraphs, primitive groups whose Saxl hypergraphs have flag-spanning tours, and appropriate generalisations of Burness and Giudici's Common Neighbour Conjecture.\n\n# The Saxl hypergraph of a permutation group\n\n  * May 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2505.13849\n\nAuthors:\n\nMelissa Lee\n\nMelissa Lee\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nAnthony Pisani\n\nAnthony Pisani\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a permutation group $G \\le \\mathrm{Sym}(\\Omega)$, a subset B of $\\Omega$ is said to be a base if its pointwise stabiliser in G is trivial, and the base size b(G) is the minimum size of a base. In the notable case $b(G) = 2$, Burness and Giudici define the Saxl graph of G to be the graph on $\\Omega$ with bases of size 2 as edges. Later work of Freedman et al. extends this notion to any group for which $b(G) \\ge 2$, taking the pairs of points contained in bases of size b(G) for edges. We study an alternative generalisation, the Saxl hypergraph, where bases of size b(G) are themselves the edges. In particular, we consider groups with complete Saxl hypergraphs, primitive groups whose Saxl hypergraphs have flag-spanning tours, and appropriate generalisations of Burness and Giudici's Common Neighbour Conjecture.\n\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (35)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nThe base size of the symmetric group acting on subsets\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Sep 2024\n\n  * Coen del Valle\n  * Colva Roney-Dougal\n\nA base for a permutation group G acting on a set Ω is a subset ℬ of Ω such that the pointwise stabiliser G (ℬ) is trivial. Let n and r be positive integers with n>2r. The symmetric and alternating groups S n and A n admit natural primitive actions on the set of r-element subsets of {1,2,⋯,n}. Building on work of Halasi [8], we provide explicit expressions for the base sizes of all of these actions, and hence determine the base size of all primitive actions of S n and A n .\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nBase sizes of primitive groups of diagonal type\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGluing posets and the dichotomy of poset saturation numbers (https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/items/9b541d1a-7650-457a-b20d-8bb1235a00f4)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month; As an application, we show that for any poset one may add at most 3 elements to to obtain a poset whose saturation number growth is at most linear: this may be viewed as a step towards the other major open problem in the area, namely the conjecture that every finite poset has growth that is at most linear. ... https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.12223\n\nJaffe, Sean\n\n## Abstract\n\nAbstract Given a finite poset , we say that a family of subsets of is ‐saturated if does not contain an induced copy of , but adding any other set to creates an induced copy of . The saturation number of is the size of the smallest ‐saturated family with ground set . The saturation number for posets is known to exhibit a dichotomy: it is either bounded or it has a growth rate of at least . Determining which posets have bounded saturation number is a major open problem. In this paper we consider a ‘gluing’ operation, formed from two finite posets and by setting all elements of to be below all elements of . We show that (under some mild assumptions) this operation preserves bounded and unbounded saturation number. This is the first such ‘new from old’ poset construction to be found. As an application, we show that for any poset one may add at most 3 elements to to obtain a poset whose saturation number growth is at most linear: this may be viewed as a step towards the other major open problem in the area, namely the conjecture that every finite poset has growth that is at most linear. We also consider the poset equivalent of weak saturation for graphs: for each finite poset , we determine exactly the minimum size of a percolating family for .\n\n## Description\n\nPublication status: Published\n\n## Keywords\n\n4901 Applied Mathematics, 4904 Pure Mathematics, 49 Mathematical Sciences\n\n## Journal Title\n\nMathematika\n\n## Journal ISSN\n\n0025-5793\n2041-7942\n\n## Volume Title\n\n72\n\n## Publisher\n\nWiley\n\n## Publisher DOI\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1112/mtk.70115\n\n## Rights and licensing\n\nExcept where otherwised noted, this item's license is described as http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\n\n#### Relationships\n\nIs derived from:\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.12223--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGluing Posets and the Dichotomy of Poset Saturation Numbers | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2503.12223)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # 2503.12223 ... As an application, we show that for any poset $\\mathcal P$ one may add at most 3 elements to $\\mathcal P$ to obtain a poset whose saturation number growth is at most linear: this may be viewed as a step towards the other major open problem in the area, namely the conjecture that every finite poset has this growth at most linear.\n# 2503.12223\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Gluing Posets and the Dichotomy of Poset Saturation Numbers [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: Maria-Romina Ivan, Sean Jaffe\n\nGiven a finite poset $\\mathcal P$, we say that a family $\\mathcal F$ of subsets of $[n]$ is $\\mathcal P$-saturated if $\\mathcal F$ does not contain an induced copy of $\\mathcal P$, but adding any other set to $\\mathcal F$ creates an induced copy of $\\mathcal P$. The saturation number of $\\mathcal P$ is the size of the smallest $\\mathcal P$-saturated family with ground set $[n]$. The saturation number for posets is known to exhibit a dichotomy: it is either bounded or it has at least $\\sqrt n$ rate of growth. Determining which posets have bounded saturation number is a major open problem. In this paper we consider a `gluing' operation, formed from two finite posets $\\mathcal P$ and $\\mathcal Q$ by setting all elements of $\\mathcal P$ to be below all elements of $\\mathcal Q$. We show that (under some mild assumptions) this operation preserves bounded and unbounded saturation number. This is the first such `new from old' poset construction to be found. As an application, we show that for any poset $\\mathcal P$ one may add at most 3 elements to $\\mathcal P$ to obtain a poset whose saturation number growth is at most linear: this may be viewed as a step towards the other major open problem in the area, namely the conjecture that every finite poset has this growth at most linear. We also consider the poset equivalent of weak saturation for graphs: for each finite poset $\\mathcal P$, we determine exactly the minimum size of a percolating family for $\\mathcal P$.\n\nSubject: Combinatorics\n\nPublish: 2025-03-15 18:17:15 UTC\n\n#1 Gluing Posets and the Dichotomy of Poset Saturation Numbers\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications | Faculty of Mathematics (https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/publications?page=18)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;   * If you specify more than one search option (e.g. you search for both \"Authors\" and \"Paper title\") then the publ